  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n- Dry through Tuesday\n\nWe're looking at some patchy dense fog again early this morning.\nHart, Fremont, and Ludington are at or near a quarter mile and a\ncouple other locations around a half mile. Any fog that does\ndevelop will burn off by mid morning.\n\nHigh pressure remains entrenched across the Great Lakes extending\nwest to the upper MS Valley and being held in place by the Mid\nAtlantic low. A light north wind will keep dewpoints comfortably \nin the 50s through Tuesday and we'll see quite a bit of sunshine; \nIR loop shows scattered high clouds to the east and west. \n\nOnce the low begins to move northeast today, the high will also\ndrift east and we should develop a south flow by Tuesday. Tuesday\nwill be another sunny day, but may see some high clouds begin to\nmove in from the west later in the day. \n\n- Showers and isolated thunder Tuesday night - Friday\n\nThe blocking low off the East Coast has delayed the arrival of the\nnext cold front until Wednesday. The GFS and ECMWF are in pretty\ngood agreement in showing showers/storms along the cold frontal\nboundary, extending to the low in western Kansas. Instability is\nvery weak with positive LI's and MUCAPE a couple hundred j/kg at\nbest Wednesday into Thursday. That said, most ECMWF ensemble\nmembers produce rain during this time frame with the average\naround a half inch. \n\nThe cold front will move south across the cwa Friday and take the\nprecipitation with it. High pressure quickly builds in behind it\nfor Saturday and much of Sunday. \n\n- Mild through Thursday, then cooler\n\nAverage highs around around 70 currently and we'll be in the mid\n70s through Thursday before cooling into the mid 60s Friday,\nbehind the cold front as h8 temps fall to around 5c from 18c\nThursday.",
